/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Leaching has three steps in series: the solvent enters the pores and the solute dissolves; the solution diffuses out through the pores to the surface; it then crosses the liquid film into the bulk liquid.
   - After a mixture of solid and solvent is left long enough, it separates into an overflow, clear solution free of inert solid, and an underflow, the inert solid plus the solution it retains.
   - Equilibrium is drawn on a diagram of N, kg inert per kg of solution, against y, the mass fraction of solute in the solution (solid-free basis).
   - I is kg inert solid; r is kg solution retained per kg inert; L is kg of solution in the underflow; AF is kg solute in the feed and WF kg solvent (liquid) already in it; S is kg of fresh solvent stream and AS the solute it brings (0 if pure)
   - Solute in the overflow is Vy and in the underflow Ly.
   - Multistage cross-current leaching: the underflow from stage 1 (I kg inert with L kg solution at y1) is mixed with a fresh portion of solvent in stage 2, and so on.
   - Stage efficiency: a real stage dissolves less solute than an ideal one when the contact time is short or the particles are thick.
*/
